        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fig. 9.1 shows an absorption spectrum for chlorophyll <i>a</i> and the corresponding action spectrum for a species of plant.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "id": "fig9.1",
            "caption": "Fig. 9.1",
            "src": "figures/q9_fig9_1.png",
            "alt": "Absorption and action spectra",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23
            }
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "a.i",
            "label": "(i)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23,
              "ms": 20
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Explain what is meant by an absorption spectrum and an action spectrum.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 23
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 2,
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "13.1(5)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 2,
              "instruction": null,
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "<i>absorption</i> <i>spectrum</i> absorption of <b>different</b> wavelengths (of light) by (named) pigment(s) <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "<i> </i> <i>action spectrum </i> <b>rate</b> of photosynthesis at different wavelengths (of light) <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "An absorption spectrum concerns light absorbed, whereas an action spectrum concerns the effectiveness of wavelengths in driving photosynthesis. Compare the same wavelengths on both curves.",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(5)",
                    "ms:a.i#1"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Identify what each vertical axis represents.",
                "Compare peaks and low regions using the wavelength scale."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Using absorption spectrum and action spectrum as interchangeable terms",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(5)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "short"
          },
          {
            "key": "a.ii",
            "label": "(ii)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 23,
              "ms": 20
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Suggest <b>and</b> explain why the curve shown in Fig. 9.1 for the absorption spectrum is different from the curve for the action spectrum for wavelengths of light between 450 nm and 550 nm.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 23
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 3,
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "13.1(4)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 3,
              "instruction": "<i>any</i><b><i> three </i></b><i>from :</i><b><i> </i></b>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "1  chlorophyll b / carotenoids / accessory pigments (are present) <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "2  (these other pigments) absorb light (energy), between 450 and 550 nm / of wavelengths that chlorophyll a does not absorb <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "3  energy passed on to, reaction centre / chlorophyll a / primary pigment <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "4  for, light-dependent reactions / photophosphorylation / photolysis (of water) / photoactivation <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "Accessory pigments absorb wavelengths that chlorophyll a absorbs less strongly and transfer excitation energy to reaction centres. Pigment absorption and whole-plant photosynthetic activity therefore need not have identical curves.",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(4)",
                    "ms:a.ii#1"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Identify which pigments can absorb the relevant wavelengths.",
                "Connect their absorption to energy transfer for photosynthesis."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Assuming that chlorophyll a is the only photosynthetic pigment",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(4)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "short"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
